  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Solve $-9{y}^{3}+27{y}^{2}+y-3=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n9{y}^{3}-27{y}^{2}-y+3=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{3}-27{y}^{2}-y+3 & = \\\\ 9{y}^{2}(y-3)-1(y-3) & = \\\\ (9{y}^{2}-1)(y-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(9{y}^{2}-1)(y-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(y-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=3,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.$",
